      <div type="clause" n="35">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Ine">Ine</ref> 35</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="35" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="104" ed="#liebermann"/>LXXVIIII. Se ðe ðeof slihð<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.5"/>, he mot<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.6-3"/> aðe gecyðan, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he hine fleondne for ðeof sloge, ⁊ þæs deadan mægas<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.7-3"/> him swerian unceases áð. Gif he hit þonn<hi rend="italic">e</hi> dierne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.8-2"/>, ⁊ sie<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.9-2"/> eft yppe, þonne forgielde<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.10-2"/> he hine.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="35" corresp="#wit-h"><pb n="104" ed="#liebermann"/>LXXVIIII. Se ðe þeof slihð, he mot aþe gecyðan, þæt he hine fleonde for ðeof sloge, ⁊ ðæs deadan magas him swerian<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.6-2"/> unceastes<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.7-2"/> áð. Gif he hit ðonne dyrne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.1"/>, ⁊ sy<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.1"/> eft yppe, ðonne forgilde he hine.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="35" corresp="#wit-b-no-prol"><pb n="104" ed="#liebermann"/>Be ðeofslihte. Se ðe ðeof slihþ, he mot mid aðe gecyðan, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he hine fleondne for ðeof sloge, ⁊ ðes deadan magas him swerian unceases að. Gyf he hit ðonne dyrne, ⁊ sy eft yppe, ðonne forgylde he hine.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="35" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="105" ed="#liebermann"/>Qui furem occiderit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.6"/>, licet<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.7"/> ei probare cum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.8"/> iuramento (iureiurando), quod eum fugientem pro fure occidit, et parentibus ipsius occisi iuret unceases<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.9"/> aþ<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.10"/> (id est sacramentum sine<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.11"/> electione<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="12" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.12"/> [uel<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.13"/> nominatione]). Si concelauerit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="14" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.14"/>, et sit<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.15"/> deinceps manifestum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#Ine_35.lnote.oe.16"/>, tunc persoluat eum.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="46" ed="#attenborough"/>[Be ðeofslihte.] Se ðe ðeof slihð, he mot aðe<ptr type="editorNote" n="17" target="#Ine_35.attnote.fn.17"/> gecyðan, þæt he hine fleondne<ptr type="editorNote" n="18" target="#Ine_35.attnote.fn.18"/> for ðeof sloge, ⁊ þæs deadan mægas him swerian unceases<ptr type="editorNote" n="19" target="#Ine_35.attnote.fn.19"/> áð. Gif he hit þonne dierne, ⁊ sie eft yppe, þonne forgielde he hine.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="35" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="400" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>{LXXVIIII} Se ðe ðeof slihð, he mot aðe gecyðan, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he hine fleonde for ðeof sloge, ⁊ þæs deadan mægas him swerian unceases áð. Gif he hit þon<hi rend="italic">ne</hi> dierne, ⁊ sie eft yppe, þonne forgielde he hine.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="105" ed="#liebermann"/>Wer einen Dieb erschlägt, der darf durch<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_35.lnote.de.1"/> Eid beweisen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Ine_35.lnote.de.2"/>, dass er ihn, den fliehenden<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_35.lnote.de.3"/>, als Dieb erschlug; und die<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_35.lnote.de.4"/> Verwandten des Erschlagenen sollen ihm Urfehde-Eid<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Ine_35.lnote.de.5"/> schwören. Wenn er es aber verbirgt, und [es] wird späterhin offenbar<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_35.lnote.de.6"/>, dann vergelte er ihn.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Whoever slays a thief may prove by oath that he slew him, as he was fleeing, as a thief; and the kinsmen of the slain man are to swear him an oath renouncing feud. But if he conceals it, and [it] becomes known later, then let him pay for him.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="47" ed="#attenborough"/>He who kills a thief shall be allowed to declare with an oath that he whom he killed was a thief trying to escape, and the kinsmen of the dead man shall swear an oath to carry on no vendetta against him. If, however, he keeps it [the homicide] secret, and it afterwards comes to light, then he shall pay for him.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><pb n="403" ed="#whitelock"/>He who slays a thief may declare with an oath that he slew him fleeing as a thief, and the kinsmen of the dead man are to swear to him an oath not to carry on a feud. If, however, he conceals it and it is revealed later, he is then to pay for him.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="401" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>He who kills a thief may declare on oath that he slew him for a thief as he was running away, and the kin of the dead man must swear him an oath not to engage in hostilities against him. If he, however, conceals [the slaying] and it afterwards becomes known, then he must make restitution for him.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="400"><label>compare</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_21">Ine 21</ref> <quote>Compare to §21.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="400"><label>equivalent phrase</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_28">Ine 28</ref> <quote>The phrase unceases áð is equivalent to the aðas unfæhða of §28.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="401"><label>compare (term)</label> <ref target="clause:Abt_7">Abt 7</ref> <quote>For ceas ("strife"), compare Æthelberht §13. This term is a variant of aðas unfæhða in §28.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="401"><label>compare</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_16">Ine 16</ref> <quote>Compare to §16 and §20.1.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="401"><label>compare</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_21">Ine 21</ref> <quote>Compare to §16 and §20.1.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="401"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_21-1">Ine 21.1</ref> <quote>This clause and Ine §20.2 are the earliest Anglo-Saxon ordinances differentiating between a killing which is open and one which is concealed.</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="45"><label>repeats (near-identical wording)</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_16">Ine 16</ref> <quote>The repetitive chapters are (in Liebermann’s numeration) “16 and 35, 28 and 35, 28 and 36, or 18 and 37.”</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="45"><label>repeats (near-identical wording)</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_28">Ine 28</ref> <quote>The repetitive chapters are (in Liebermann’s numeration) “16 and 35, 28 and 35, 28 and 36, or 18 and 37.”</quote></note>
